    "EQ-2026-007": {
      "title": "Equipment Replacement Guidelines",
      "text": "CF SUMMIT 2026 | SYNTHETIC DEMO DATA | NOT A REAL COMPANY POLICY\nPage 1\nEquipment Replacement Guidelines\nInternational Business Travel\nDocument ID\nEQ-2026-007\nTenant\ncompany-a\nVersion\n3.0\nStatus\nAPPROVED\nEffective from\n2026-01-01\nEffective through\nUntil superseded\nAudience\nall-staff\nCurrent\ntrue\n1. Purpose and scope\nThis policy applies to company-issued computing equipment used by Company A employees during approved\ninternational business travel. It governs damage reporting and replacement eligibility. It does not guarantee\nreplacement or reimbursement.\n2. Eligibility, conditions and exclusions\nEmployees may request a replacement for company-issued computing equipment damaged during approved\ninternational business travel. The incident must be reported to IT within 24 hours after discovery, and\nreplacement requires IT approval. Personally owned devices are not covered. These conditions apply together;\ntravel approval alone does not authorize replacement.\n3. Reporting procedure\nContact the IT Service Desk through the incident portal. Provide the asset tag, time of discovery, description of\nthe damage and approved travel reference. Attach photographs when available. If the device is unsafe, stop\nusing it and follow the Service Desk instructions. Do not purchase replacement equipment before IT approval.\n4. Replacement and availability\nIT verifies the assigned asset, confirms coverage and decides whether repair, a loan device or replacement is\nappropriate. Inventory availability is checked in the live asset system. This document contains no current stock\ncount. An available device is not proof that replacement has been approved.\n5. Authority and version\nThis approved policy supersedes EQ-2023-004 version 1.0 for incidents on or after 2026-01-01. The former\n72-hour reporting rule does not apply to those incidents. Travel guidance describes workflow; this policy\ngoverns equipment eligibility. Policy owner: IT Operations. No approved 2027 reimbursement limit is provided in\nthis document.\n",
      "sha256": "6f524aadb335f305ee126f304dfb9e268c3b1c21cdef5e82be9c35fb68a39ca8"
    },
    "TR-2026-002": {
      "title": "Business Travel Guidelines",
      "text": "CF SUMMIT 2026 | SYNTHETIC DEMO DATA | NOT A REAL COMPANY POLICY\nPage 1\nBusiness Travel Guidelines\nEquipment incidents during international assignments\nDocument ID\nTR-2026-002\nTenant\ncompany-a\nVersion\n2.0\nStatus\nAPPROVED\nEffective from\n2026-01-01\nEffective through\nUntil superseded\nAudience\nall-staff\nCurrent\ntrue\n1. Approved assignments\nAn overseas client assignment must have a travel approval reference recorded by the employee's manager.\nThe travel record confirms the assignment dates and destination. It does not grant permission to buy or replace\ncomputing equipment.\n2. Damaged equipment abroad\nFor damage to company-issued computing equipment during approved international travel, report the incident\nto IT within 24 hours after discovery. Follow Equipment Replacement Guidelines EQ-2026-007 for eligibility,\nexclusions and IT approval. Notify the travel coordinator so client commitments can be adjusted.\n3. Required information\nInclude the approved travel reference, asset tag, incident location and discovery time in the IT incident. The\ntravel coordinator can help locate travel records but cannot approve equipment replacement.\n4. Personal property and costs\nPersonally owned computing devices are outside the company equipment replacement process. Do not assume\nthat a travel booking or client visit makes personal equipment eligible. Follow EX-2026-003 for expense\nsubmission; no reimbursement amount is established here.\n5. Ownership\nPolicy owner: Travel Operations. This guideline supports EQ-2026-007 and does not change its conditions.\nCurrent asset assignments and replacement stock must be read from the live application.\n",
      "sha256": "d44f4cb1e2831ee19436c7ee849e2724bfc09cf483cc4703460003bddb58e448"
    },
